{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfpnNWQhWFbosi2bCJtr5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`vi.mock` isn't a normal runtime call. Vitest moves it to the top of the test file at compile time so the mock is registered before any `import` runs. Putting it inside `beforeEach` doesn't change that: it still runs once, before your tests, not before each test. Newer Vitest versions treat a non-top-level `vi.mock` as an error, while older ones only warned.\n\nThe other common cause is the factory function. If it uses variables declared in the `beforeEach` scope or elsewhere in the file, those variables don't exist yet when the moved call runs. That gives you a `ReferenceError` or a \"There was an error when mocking a module\" message. You can share values with the factory safely by creating them with `vi.hoisted()`.\n\nThe fix depends on what you need:\n- **Set up the mock once, change its behavior per test:** call `vi.mock(...)` at the top level. Then in `beforeEach`, use `vi.mocked(fn).mockReturnValue(...)` or `mockReset()`.\n- **Swap in a different mock module per test:** use `vi.doMock(...)`, which isn't moved. It only affects imports that happen after it, so call `vi.resetModules()` and then `await import(...)` the module inside the test or hook."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2450,"cache_read_input_tokens":1693,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2450},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21efa76e-0961-480d-8519-6a8ad6f36ad8","uuid":"d0472870-9f05-4565-aee4-4ba43f36f96b","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:38:30.505Z","request_id":"req_011CfpnNW7qYXKri7eonDW64"}
